I sent this much my way.

46:37.200 --> 46:38.920
We're gonna either wash it out

46:38.920 --> 46:41.520
or you owe me money or I owe you
money

46:41.520 --> 46:45.040
and these peering agreements
started basically by saying,

46:45.040 --> 46:46.040
look, we can't count it

46:46.040 --> 46:48.560
but we can't see exactly what's
coming through you

46:48.560 --> 46:49.800
or is directed towards you

46:49.800 --> 46:52.160
but it's kind of the same amount
so we're peers.

46:52.160 --> 46:54.840
And so yes, I think the only way
to do it

46:54.840 --> 46:57.880
is to go back to actual packet
counting.

46:57.880 --> 46:58.720
At a cost.

46:58.720 --> 47:00.440
I mean, that means all of a
sudden

47:00.440 --> 47:02.080
you're putting switches and
routers everywhere.

47:02.080 --> 47:03.000
You're measuring everything.

47:03.000 --> 47:05.320
There's some bottlenecking.

47:05.320 --> 47:06.320
At the end of the day though,

47:06.320 --> 47:08.520
I think the consumer will win
big time.

47:08.520 --> 47:10.040
I think exactly the opposite.

47:10.040 --> 47:13.800
The consumer at home is going to
get fantastic throughput

47:13.800 --> 47:15.120
from anything they want.

47:16.240 --> 47:17.320
Well, you know, it's funny.

47:17.320 --> 47:18.560
If they're willing to pay for it

47:18.560 --> 47:19.920
and their ISP is willing to pay
for it.

47:19.920 --> 47:21.120
Well, here's a, I mean,

47:21.120 --> 47:23.520
we have listeners in Sweden who
continually tell me,

47:23.520 --> 47:26.640
oh, hey, we get 100 megabit
downloads

47:26.640 --> 47:27.840
for 20 bucks a month.

47:27.840 --> 47:30.040
Why is it so cheap there?

47:30.040 --> 47:31.720
It's subsidized by the
government.

47:31.720 --> 47:33.480
The government's paying for it.

47:33.480 --> 47:34.800
Is that what you wanted, Will?

47:34.800 --> 47:35.640
Thank you.

47:35.640 --> 47:36.480
Sure.

47:36.480 --> 47:38.920
And I think our government
should pitch in too.

47:38.920 --> 47:41.480
In fact, I believe that's part
of the net neutrality debate

47:41.480 --> 47:43.960
is that there was lots of money
allocated

47:43.960 --> 47:45.920
towards the telcos years ago

47:45.920 --> 47:47.760
and they were supposed to build
out their networks

47:47.760 --> 47:48.680
and they didn't really do it.

47:48.680 --> 47:52.840
And I think that's where a lot
of anger stems from.

47:52.840 --> 47:54.400
But yeah, this is just important
to me

47:54.400 --> 47:57.280
as the electricity grid or the
water network.

47:57.280 --> 48:00.280
Why wouldn't the government at
least have some involvement

48:00.280 --> 48:02.280
